resources cite91†Geography resources cite92†STEM Resources cite93†Facts and figures cite94†Famous writers L30: \nL31: Back cite30†Plan your visit cite95†Getting to and around the Lake District cite96†Lake District weather cite97†Plan your winter visit cite98†What to pack cite99†Rights of way and access cite100†Accessible for all cite101†Countryside Code L32: Back cite31†Things to do cite102†Walking cite103†Cycling cite104†Miles without Stiles cite105†On the water cite106†Historical places cite107†Families and children cite108†Winter Skills day courses L33: \nL34: Back cite32†Places to go cite109†Explore Southern Lakes cite110†Explore Grasmere and Rydal cite111†Explore Langdale Valley cite112†Explore Northern Lakes cite113†Explore Ullswater cite114†Explore Wasdale, Wastwater and West Coast cite115†Explore Windermere and Ambleside cite116†Explore by map L35: Back cite33†Where to stay cite117†Camping sites cite118†Wild camping cite119†Glamping and camping pods cite120†Caravan sites cite121†Campervans and where to park cite122†Hostels and bunkhouses L36: \nL37: Back cite34†Visitor information cite123†Information Centres cite124†Coniston Boating Centre cite125†Wheelchair and mobility scooter hire cite126†Car parks and parking cite127†Electric car hire and charging points cite128†Public toilets cite129†Webcams, videos and photos cite130†FAQs L38: \nL39: Menu Close\nL40: ## A land carved from rock\nL41: \nL42: In the Lake District, 500 million years of geological processes have produced a physical landscape of mountains and lakes of great scenic beauty. The layers of rock formed were shifted and sculpted – first through different stages of folding and uplifting and then by the actions of glaciers and meltwater.\nL43: Slate developed from sediments in oceans and seas, volcanoes erupted, limestone was formed by the deposition of dead crustaceans and sandstone was created in desert conditions. Various minerals were also formed in joints and faults in the bedrock.\nL44: As a result, the topography of the Lake District includes smooth U-shaped valleys and steep and sharp ridges, England’s highest mountain and deepest and longest lakes. The Lake District is often compared to a wheel, with the hub approximately at Dunmail Raise, north of Grasmere. The valleys and lakes radiate outwards as the ‘spokes’.\nL45: ## Lake District geology\nL46: \nL47: #### Skiddaw Group\nL48: \nL49: The Skiddaw Group are the oldest rocks in the Lake District. They were formed as black muds and sands settling on the seabed about 500 million years ago. They have since been raised up and crumpled and squeezed. These rocks are found mainly in the north of the National Park and the mountains they form are mostly smooth, though many streams have cut deep gorges.\nL50: #### Borrowdale Volcanic Group\nL51: \nL52: Found in the central Lake District, the ‘Borrowdale Volcanics’ are very hard lavas and ashes formed in catastrophic eruptions about 450 million years ago. They make up the highest and craggiest mountains: Scafell, Helvellyn and the Langdale Pikes.\nL53: \nL54: These peaks are not the remains of the original volcanoes – they are the harder volcanic rocks that have withstood erosion.\nL55: #### Windermere Group\nL56: \nL57: Sedimentary mudstones, sandstones, siltstones and some limestone formed in the sea about 420 million years ago. These were later folded and faulted, pushed up, and eroded down to their present levels former the gentler scenery of southern Lakeland.\nL58: \nL59: #### Granite\nL60: \nL61: Huge masses of granite formed about 400 million years ago deep below the Lake District. Erosion has revealed outcrops in Eskdale, Ennerdale and at Shap.\nL62: #### Limestone\nL63: \nL64: 320 million years ago a tropical sea covered the Lake District. The shell remains of incredible numbers of small animals formed limestone which is visible at Whitbarrow Scar and Scout Scar in the south of the National Park.\nL65: \nL66: #### Honister\nL67: \nL68: cite131†Honister, a U-shaped valley carved out of volcanic rock by glacial action. Image credit: Nick Thorne L69: \nL70: * * *\nL71: ## What is a catchment?\nL72: \nL73: Many people have heard of “school catchment areas” meaning the areas of towns and villages from which schools recruit their pupils.\nL74: \nL75: ‘Catchment’ means a similar thing when referring to ecology and geography. In this context, a catchment is the area of land, including the hills and mountains, woodlands, and buildings which water drains from, before flowing into the streams, rivers, lakes and tarns.\nL76: ### Windermere catchment\nL77: \nL78: For example, the Windermere Catchment includes rivers such as the Rothay, Brathay and Troutbeck, as well as tarns and lakes including Grasmere, Rydal Water, Esthwaite and Elterwater.\nL79: \nL80: The map below shows the catchment area in a light coloured area with a dark grey boundary:\nL81: \nL82: cite132†Image L83: \nL84: * * *\nL85: \nL86: ## Useful links\nL87: \nL88: Geology factsheet\nL89: \nL90: PDF\nL91: \nL92: Cumberland Geological Society\nL93: \nL94: Website\nL95: \nL96: Westmorland Geological Society\nL97: \nL98: Website\nL99: \nL100: Cumbria Geoconservation\nL101: \nL102: Website\nL103: ## More information\nL104: \nL105: cite133†Image: picture of Bassenthwaite Lake reflecting green and orange fells at sunset, leafless shadowed tree on lakeshore in foreground L106: \nL107: ### Lake District National Park facts and figures\nL108: \nL109: cite134†Image: people standing on a path next to rocks L110: \nL111: ### Learning resource: Geology, rocks and minerals\nL112: \nL113: cite135†Image: Picture of Wasdale Valley a mountain landscape with flat valley bottom L114: \nL115: ### Geography resource: Mountain landscape\nL116: \nL117: cite136†Image: Image showing a world class cultural landscape L118: ### Local Pla